---
import RegisterStructureDemo from "../../components/RegisterStructureDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import StructureLessonLayout from "../../components/StructureLessonLayout.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import { registerMapTerm } from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<StructureLessonLayout
  title="RegisterMap"
  description="The ranger stores several named station values and prevents stale reports from replacing accepted atomic values."
  articleClass="register-lesson"
  illustrationId="structures-register-map"
>
    <Fragment slot="summary">
      The ranger keeps a record for each station field: trail status, radio
      channel, gate code, and more. Alice and Bob submit conflicting trail
      reports without seeing each other. Carol updates the radio channel at
      the same time. The ranger sequences all three writes without letting
      the conflict on one field disturb the others.
    </Fragment>

    <StructureFacts
      structure="RegisterMap"
      family="DDS"
      model="Sequenced map of named registers"
      updates="Non-optimistic writes to named cells"
      merge="Retain sequenced versions; read as atomic or latest."
      delivery="One server-ordered operation stream"
      bestFor="Named values where stale writers must not replace an accepted atomic value"
      cost="Retained versions and sequence metadata for each active key"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="use-title">
      <h2 id="use-title">One shared record, several independent fields</h2>
      <p>
        A <dfn><a href="/glossary/#registermap">RegisterMap</a></dfn>
        {" "}stores a register under each key. A station application can keep
        <code>trail-status</code>, <code>radio-channel</code>, and
        <code>gate-code</code> in one structure while the kernel resolves
        writes for each key separately.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...registerMapTerm} />
      <p>
        This matters when a client can submit a write from an old view. A
        normal last-writer-wins map would let any later-sequenced write replace
        the earlier value. RegisterMap can retain that later write without
        treating it as the atomic value. A reader can ask for the protected
        atomic value or the most recently sequenced version.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="versions-title">
      <h2 id="versions-title">The sequence numbers record what a writer knew</h2>
      <p>
        Each write carries a reference sequence number: the last accepted
        sequence number that the writer had observed. The sequencer then gives
        the write its own sequence number. These two numbers let the kernel
        distinguish a newer informed write from a later-arriving stale write.
      </p>
      <figure class="sequence-example">
        <table aria-label="RegisterMap read policies">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Write</th>
              <th scope="col">Last sequence seen at submit</th>
              <th scope="col">Assigned sequence</th>
              <th scope="col">Atomic read</th>
              <th scope="col">Latest read</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Alice: trail-status = Trail open</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Trail open</td><td>Trail open</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bob: trail-status = Trail closed</th><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>Trail open</td><td>Trail closed</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Carol: radio-channel = Channel 4</th><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>Channel 4</td><td>Channel 4</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Carol: trail-status = Inspect bridge</th><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>Inspect bridge</td><td>Inspect bridge</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>
          The two read columns apply to the key in that row. A conflict on
          <code>trail-status</code> does not affect <code>radio-channel</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Alice's write creates the atomic value at sequence <code>1</code>.
        Bob's write arrives later, but its reference is still <code>0</code>.
        It becomes the latest version at sequence <code>2</code>, but it
        cannot replace the atomic value. Carol's radio update creates a
        separate register under another key.
      </p>
      <p>
        After Carol has observed sequence <code>3</code>, she can submit
        <code>Inspect bridge</code> for <code>trail-status</code>. Her
        reference covers the current atomic winner, so the write at sequence
        <code>4</code> becomes both the atomic and latest value. The kernel
        also removes the older versions that Carol had observed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RegisterStructureDemo
      kind="register-map"
      title="Protect one field while another changes"
      description="Alice and Bob race stale trail reports while Carol updates radio-channel. Then Carol submits a caught-up trail-status write."
      rule="Atomic changes when a writer has seen its current winner; latest uses the greatest sequence number."
      raceLabel="Sequence the three station writes"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="choice-title">
      <h2 id="choice-title">The reader must choose a policy</h2>
      <p>
        After the first three writes, an atomic read of
        <code>trail-status</code> returns Alice's accepted value. A latest
        read returns Bob's later stale report. Both policies return
        <code>Channel 4</code> for <code>radio-channel</code>. Use the
        caught-up-write control to let Carol replace the atomic trail status.
      </p>
      <p>
        RegisterMap is closest in shape to a map of registers, but its
        reference sequence number adds conditional-write behavior. Neither
        read policy merges values or exposes causal siblings like an
        MvRegister.
      </p>
      <aside class="term-callout" aria-labelledby="dots-title">
        <h3 class="term-callout-label" id="dots-title">Alternative perspective</h3>
        <p>
          The assigned sequence number identifies one accepted version, and
          the reference sequence number summarizes the accepted history that
          the writer saw. Together they serve a role similar to a CRDT dot and
          causal context. RegisterMap can use plain integers because one
          sequencer supplies a single global order.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p>
        This is a sequenced distributed data structure, not a CRDT. If the
        sequencer is unavailable, new writes cannot receive the ordering
        evidence that both read policies require.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="source-title">
      <h2 id="source-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/register_collection_kernel.gleam"><code>register_collection_kernel</code></a>
        {" "}defines sequenced writes and atomic and latest read policies.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Implementation:</strong> The demo calls the kernel through the Gleam toolkit
        to write two keys, sequence three clients' operations, apply remote
        changes, and inspect the atomic and latest trail-status versions.
      </p>
    </section>

</StructureLessonLayout>

<style>
  .sequence-example {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .sequence-example table {
    min-width: 42rem;
  }
</style>
